WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.009
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:02.460
Today

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:06.540
I want to talk about learning
Chinese or Mandarin Chinese

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:10.770
versus, uh, Japanese and Korean.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:14.520
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.500
on the bell to get notifications.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:25.260
So, uh, I have learned to varying degrees
of fluency, uh, Mandarin Chinese, which

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.680
is the first one I learned back in 1968.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:33.105
Full-time at school, but living in
an environment which was not Mandarin

00:00:33.105 --> 00:00:35.144
speaking, in other words, Hong Kong.

00:00:36.135 --> 00:00:41.714
Uh, then I learned Japanese basically
on my own, uh, helped by the fact

00:00:41.714 --> 00:00:43.394
that I could read Chinese characters.

00:00:43.724 --> 00:00:46.724
And of course, living in Japan
for nine years, my Japanese

00:00:46.724 --> 00:00:48.254
got progressively better.

00:00:48.435 --> 00:00:54.165
And then I said, at some point, I think in
the early eighties, I should learn Korean

00:00:54.165 --> 00:00:59.519
because I have invested so much time
into learning, uh, Chinese and Japanese,

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:01.200
but I never had much time for it.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.870
And at different sort of
points I have given it a go.

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:08.850
Three months here, six months there.

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:14.910
And I'm really not very satisfied with
the level that I have achieved in Korean,

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.759
but let's compare these three languages.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:25.590
So they're East Asian languages from a
geographic point of view because these

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:27.660
three countries are located in East Asia.

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:31.890
However, they are not of
the same language family.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:35.530
Chinese is Sino-Tibetan.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:42.300
So related to languages in Tibet,
Burma, uh, I don't know all of them.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:46.590
I'm not a linguist in the sense of being,
you know, someone who studies linguistics.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:54.370
Of course Japanese, according to some is
related to the sort of, uh,  Ural-Altaic

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:58.020
languages, Korean, Turkish, Mongolian.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:00.750
There are other people who challenged
that, but it's a completely

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:04.070
different language family, both
of them, Korean and Japanese.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:13.080
Um, but the underpinning of Japanese and
Chinese in terms of vocabulary is Chinese.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.790
Uh, in the case of Japanese, even their
writing system, their phonetic writing

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:21.350
system derives from Chinese characters.

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:24.210
So China had a major influence on them.

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:28.260
Of course, learning these languages
has become easier than ever.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:32.670
I learned Chinese and Japanese
at a time when there was no

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:35.640
internet, there were no MP3 files.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.359
There is no way, no online dictionaries.

00:02:38.359 --> 00:02:41.519
You certainly couldn't do the things
we do at LingQ, import stuff from

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:51.470
newspapers, from YouTube, from Netflix,
or, um, you know, basically save words

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:54.620
to a database for later review and
all of these things weren't available.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:58.279
You had a book, you looked things up
in a dictionary, and if you had the

00:02:58.279 --> 00:02:59.899
money, you got yourself a teacher.

00:03:00.285 --> 00:03:02.445
And if you are lucky enough
to live where the language was

00:03:02.445 --> 00:03:06.255
spoken, as I did for Japanese,
then of course you took advantage.

00:03:06.705 --> 00:03:09.825
I learn languages because
I'm interested in history.

00:03:10.695 --> 00:03:14.655
And so it's important to understand
that what we talk about today as

00:03:14.685 --> 00:03:20.835
China, Korea, Japan, and in the case
of China, at one point, whatever,

00:03:20.925 --> 00:03:29.385
four or five billion people, uh,
Japan, maybe 125/127 million people.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:34.710
Uh, Korea would be 50 plus 25, I
don't know the exact number for

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:36.780
North Korea, 75 million people.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:39.720
Those are large language groups.

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:47.460
And as I said, Chinese, insofar as
vocabulary is concerned, is the base.

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:52.260
More than half of all of the words
used in Chinese and Japanese,

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:57.030
especially in more serious content,
much as is the case in English where

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:59.400
much of our more sort of serious...

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:06.960
okay, academic vocabulary comes
from French in the case of Japanese

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:08.610
and Korean comes from Chinese.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:12.780
So geographically, we consider East
Asia as an area where you look at

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:18.120
China, but it's important to remember
that, you know, homosapians came out

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:24.390
of Africa, spread all over the world,
moved eastward towards, uh, Asia.

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:29.670
Maybe to some extent and increasingly
people feel there were some, you

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:37.080
know, intermingling with, uh,
previous, um, call it homosapiens.

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:39.360
Like we are homosapien sapiens.

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:43.290
So, you know, Neanderthal or
the equivalent in Asia might

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:45.800
have had its contribution.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:48.090
I just don't know, but it's possible.

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:52.485
Because things they said were not the case
before it turns out they do more research

00:04:52.485 --> 00:05:00.435
and they discover for example, that, uh,
many human or homosapian sapians did mix

00:05:00.435 --> 00:05:03.645
with Neanderthals, um, at any rate...

00:05:03.855 --> 00:05:08.175
so if you look at a map of, of human
movements 50,000 years ago, you see

00:05:08.175 --> 00:05:11.985
people moving through China, moving
from the North, down to the South.

00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:17.385
Uh, even the Ainu supposedly, sort
of indigenous to the, to Japan.

00:05:17.565 --> 00:05:19.815
They came over from the mainland.

00:05:20.475 --> 00:05:23.265
Uh, there's no one indigenous to Japan.

00:05:23.265 --> 00:05:25.605
They didn't spring out of
the ground like mushrooms.

00:05:25.605 --> 00:05:26.985
They came from somewhere.

00:05:27.345 --> 00:05:29.685
They might've come via a sea route.

00:05:29.745 --> 00:05:34.755
And there are theories that people went
to Japan via Shanghai and originated

00:05:34.755 --> 00:05:38.445
in Southern China, or that they
came across their Korean peninsula.

00:05:38.445 --> 00:05:40.635
This is 2000 odd years ago.

00:05:41.955 --> 00:05:44.355
So at some point things
sort of solidified.

00:05:44.685 --> 00:05:50.175
And there is the sense that at least in
terms of, uh, you know, genetics, not

00:05:50.175 --> 00:05:56.805
that that matters, but, uh, Japanese
with their, Ainu sort of mixture

00:05:57.164 --> 00:06:02.865
and the Koreans and the Mongolians
and the Manchurians, uh, there...

00:06:02.925 --> 00:06:06.885
and the Northern Chinese there
sort of one group, Tibetans,

00:06:06.974 --> 00:06:10.695
even though the Chinese and the
buttons are closer, linguistically.

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:15.910
And then the Southern Chinese,
Southeast Asians, that's sort of

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:20.700
like somewhat different groups
with a lot of scrambling going on.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:24.950
And of course, historically a lot
of those peoples in central or in,

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:31.930
in inner Asia call it, Mongolians
and the Khitans and the Tanguts and

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:38.160
the Manchus and the Jurchens , they
were quite powerful in their own

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:38.580
right.

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:41.475
And at various times
conquered they're neighbors.

00:06:41.475 --> 00:06:46.785
And so lots went on before we
ended up with what we got today.

00:06:47.415 --> 00:06:50.205
So that's just a little,
I like that kind of stuff.

00:06:50.535 --> 00:06:54.165
And so then of course, uh, over
the last few thousand years, these

00:06:54.165 --> 00:06:58.455
countries have developed distinctive,
you know, histories of their own.

00:06:58.455 --> 00:07:02.565
And they developed a language of
their own each one of them and

00:07:02.565 --> 00:07:04.035
a writing system of their own.

00:07:04.335 --> 00:07:10.110
And in the case of Chinese, the writing
system is exclusively pictograms,

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:13.890
each character represents meaning.

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:21.030
Not always identifiable in terms of
sort of pictoral or image wise, although

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:26.820
you can with explanations, see how it's
derived from something that we could

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:29.880
visualize, but mostly it's, it's an idea.

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:31.180
So it's an ideogram.

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:33.599
Yeah, I guess that's
the way to describe it.

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:41.640
And most of those ideograms have a
phonetic component, uh, and that's

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:45.719
where the kanna that's used Japanese...

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:51.659
in japanese they have these ideograms,
but they also have two parallel phonetic

00:07:51.659 --> 00:07:58.185
scripts, which derive from certain
Chinese characters, which were used

00:07:58.185 --> 00:08:04.145
to represent sounds in Japanese and
then eventually evolved into kanna.

00:08:04.165 --> 00:08:10.695
And this happened about, you
know, 980 or so, uh, part of that

00:08:10.695 --> 00:08:16.005
whole range of influence that came
over from China, uh, artistic,

00:08:16.005 --> 00:08:17.505
religious, literary, and so forth.

00:08:18.164 --> 00:08:22.245
Uh, in the case of Korea, somewhere
around the 15th century, supposedly

00:08:22.245 --> 00:08:23.594
their King created Hangul,

00:08:23.594 --> 00:08:28.414
which is, um, a phonetic writing system
that started from scratch and has no

00:08:28.414 --> 00:08:30.635
influence from Chinese characters.

00:08:31.474 --> 00:08:33.694
So that's in so far as the
writing system is concerned.

00:08:34.595 --> 00:08:42.004
Um, as far as, you know, the, the,
uh, Romanization or the phonetic

00:08:42.875 --> 00:08:47.345
writing system that we can use to
get a sense of how these words are

00:08:47.345 --> 00:08:51.214
pronounced when we don't yet know
Hangul or we don't yet know Hiragana,

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:58.420
uh, the Chinese use Pinyin, which was
developed, uh, you know, after 1949

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:02.660
and replaced previous systems that
had been created by, by foreigners.

00:09:03.140 --> 00:09:08.060
Uh, personally I thought that Yale
Romanization system was better because

00:09:08.089 --> 00:09:15.560
in Pinyin they use letters like Q
and X in ways that we are used to.

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:19.400
So Q has a "ch" sound.

00:09:20.119 --> 00:09:23.839
Because theoretically, the "ch" in
Chinese is a little different from "ch".

00:09:24.589 --> 00:09:25.879
So they've got a different symbol.

00:09:25.879 --> 00:09:30.480
The similarly, the X is a "shi" Xi,
which is different from S kind of

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:33.650
between "Sh" and S, so use an X.

00:09:33.889 --> 00:09:37.230
So if you get a name card from a
Chinese person and they've got Qs

00:09:37.250 --> 00:09:40.750
and Xs in there, you'll be quite
confused until you get used to it.

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:43.990
But once you get used to it, it's
actually quite a practical system.

00:09:45.579 --> 00:09:48.639
The Japanese use a Romanization system.

00:09:49.425 --> 00:09:55.474
There's two, there's the one that is
the most helpful is one where, you know,

00:09:55.474 --> 00:10:00.704
if you have a word like hatchimitsu,
which is, um, honey, uh, then the hatchi

00:10:00.704 --> 00:10:04.984
, chi is CH, and Mitsu "tsu" is TSU.

00:10:05.825 --> 00:10:11.155
However, in the Japanese writing
system, in Hiragana or Katakana,

00:10:12.075 --> 00:10:14.555
uh, because you have sort of.

00:10:14.974 --> 00:10:18.994
Sets of these...these are syllables.

00:10:19.385 --> 00:10:22.125
So you have sets of ka, ki, ku, ke, ko.

00:10:22.395 --> 00:10:23.895
Ha, hi, hu, he, ho.

00:10:24.244 --> 00:10:32.145
And then it goes, uh, Ta that uses
the T sound TA, ta, chi, tsu, te, to.

00:10:33.214 --> 00:10:38.095
So in the Japanese system, the
T becomes a "chi" and a "tsu".

00:10:38.224 --> 00:10:41.555
So they, they decide that therefore
for the poor foreigner, trying

00:10:41.555 --> 00:10:43.194
to pronounce the word hatchimitsu

00:10:43.504 --> 00:10:50.505
we should write it to be pure H A
T I M I T U, Hatimitu, which might

00:10:50.524 --> 00:10:56.014
be, you know, close to the Japanese
system but it's useless for a

00:10:56.014 --> 00:11:00.395
non-Japanese speaker trying to figure
out how to pronounce a Japanese word.

00:11:01.474 --> 00:11:05.915
Fortunately, that's not used
too much except by the purists.

00:11:06.605 --> 00:11:12.635
Now in Korean, they have a Romanization
system, which I find the least useful

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:16.700
because it uses the letter E
in all kinds of different ways.

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:21.950
And I'm never clear whether this is a,
some kind of a dipthong or an attempt

00:11:21.950 --> 00:11:30.170
to, to, uh, represent, you know,
uh, this, "uh", sound, eh, I never

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:31.220
know quite what it's trying to do.

00:11:31.220 --> 00:11:39.680
And sometimes it seems to have a Y, uh,
role whenever I see the, the, uh, Korean,

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:42.575
uh, Romanization, I'd just ignore it.

00:11:42.635 --> 00:11:46.325
I, if I'm reading on the
internet, I'll use text to speech,

00:11:46.744 --> 00:11:48.064
uh, or I'll use the Hangul.

00:11:48.514 --> 00:11:52.385
So it's really not very helpful,
but again, developed by, you

00:11:52.385 --> 00:11:53.915
know, the ministry of education.

00:11:53.915 --> 00:11:57.484
So a whole bunch of very serious people
came up with this thing as somehow being,

00:11:57.994 --> 00:12:01.015
uh, I dunno, close to the original Korean.

00:12:01.204 --> 00:12:06.425
So that's so far as the writing system
and the, uh, call it transliteration

00:12:06.425 --> 00:12:08.254
or Romanization systems used.

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:15.110
In any case, I think you have to listen,
you can't rely on the, uh, Romanization.

00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:19.880
You have to get yourself used to
seeing something written with their

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:23.690
phonetic script, which doesn't exist in
Chinese and then how it's pronounced.

00:12:24.050 --> 00:12:26.569
And if you do enough listening,
you gradually get used to

00:12:26.569 --> 00:12:28.160
it in the case of all three.

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:29.930
So that's...

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:33.650
we've covered the writing systems,
the differences, the romanticization

00:12:33.650 --> 00:12:36.889
differences, um, Pronunciation.

00:12:37.399 --> 00:12:40.940
Obviously the biggest problem
in Chinese is the tones.

00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:45.530
And there are no tones in Japanese
or Korean because remember

00:12:45.530 --> 00:12:50.060
Japanese and Korean belong to
a different language family.

00:12:51.349 --> 00:12:53.659
So tonal language is Thai,

00:12:53.659 --> 00:12:58.250
you know, Vietnamese,
Tibetan, I guess I'm assuming.

00:12:58.250 --> 00:13:00.169
Cause Sino-Tibetan, whatever.

00:13:00.470 --> 00:13:04.030
But Japanese and Korean are not tonal.

00:13:04.625 --> 00:13:09.935
So to get the tones, I found it very
difficult to try to remember the

00:13:10.115 --> 00:13:14.555
individual tones, um, with your reading.

00:13:14.555 --> 00:13:17.285
If you're reading on LingQ, the
tones are marked, a lot of places

00:13:17.285 --> 00:13:18.665
they will give you marks, you know.

00:13:19.464 --> 00:13:24.055
So they'll show you what tone it is.

00:13:24.055 --> 00:13:29.005
And by the end of reading a lot,
in Pinyin, with the tone marks, you

00:13:29.015 --> 00:13:30.474
gradually start to get used to it.

00:13:30.984 --> 00:13:33.325
But the bigger thing is
do a lot of listening.

00:13:33.619 --> 00:13:37.639
Focus on phrases so that these
natural phrases come out with the

00:13:37.639 --> 00:13:41.450
natural tones and, and recognize
that you're going to start out at

00:13:41.450 --> 00:13:45.679
30% accuracy moved to 40, 50, 60, 70.

00:13:45.679 --> 00:13:48.319
You'll never be a hundred, but
you'll get closer and closer.

00:13:49.069 --> 00:13:51.980
Uh, so tone is not a problem in Japanese.

00:13:52.129 --> 00:13:56.030
However, there are people who
claim that there is this, uh,

00:13:56.030 --> 00:13:57.980
pitch tone issue in Japanese.

00:13:58.040 --> 00:14:01.339
I have, I wasn't even aware
of it when I lived in Japan.

00:14:02.075 --> 00:14:03.905
Nobody has trouble understanding me.

00:14:04.534 --> 00:14:08.704
I don't think it's an important issue
unless you are some kind of a purist

00:14:08.704 --> 00:14:12.925
that you want to, you know, be a
hundred percent like a native, but

00:14:12.925 --> 00:14:16.435
then, then the issue becomes which
pitch tone are you going to copy?

00:14:16.435 --> 00:14:19.494
Because the pitch seems to me
quite different in Osaka than

00:14:19.494 --> 00:14:21.835
it is in Sendai or in Tokyo.

00:14:21.835 --> 00:14:26.935
So I think it's more important to pick up
on, and this is true for all languages,

00:14:26.935 --> 00:14:32.105
pick up on the natural intonation, the
natural rhythm, uh, of the language.

00:14:32.254 --> 00:14:35.375
Try not to introduce, for
example, English intonation into

00:14:35.375 --> 00:14:38.304
Japanese or Korean: "watashi wa".

00:14:38.645 --> 00:14:39.854
They don't say that.

00:14:39.854 --> 00:14:43.994
It's a little more monotonous, more like
French, and you have to catch, you know,

00:14:44.014 --> 00:14:49.264
you get ahold of that intonation by, by
dint of doing a lot of listening without

00:14:49.264 --> 00:14:53.284
trying to worry about is this sound,
which pitch and stuff, at least for

00:14:53.284 --> 00:14:55.595
me, not something I spend any time on.

00:14:55.835 --> 00:14:59.794
I would far rather increase my
vocabulary, increase my comprehension.

00:15:00.244 --> 00:15:04.835
And in doing so I will increase my
ability to communicate effectively.

00:15:05.675 --> 00:15:09.665
Uh, Korean I found that the
pronunciation actually is quite

00:15:09.665 --> 00:15:12.575
similar to, uh, to Japanese.

00:15:12.845 --> 00:15:13.984
Of course, the words are different.

00:15:13.984 --> 00:15:19.234
In fact, some words sound the same
and are completely different from

00:15:19.264 --> 00:15:22.595
Japanese, but I find this, the
pronunciation is if you can pronounce

00:15:22.595 --> 00:15:23.915
the one, you can pronounce the other.

00:15:24.305 --> 00:15:28.895
And in terms of European languages,
You know, Greek, Spanish, any of these

00:15:28.895 --> 00:15:33.635
languages where you have few diphthongs,
basically pure vowels, it's going to

00:15:33.635 --> 00:15:35.345
be similar to those two languages.

00:15:36.605 --> 00:15:36.875
Okay.

00:15:36.875 --> 00:15:40.985
Grammar, generally speaking,
having studied Slavic languages,

00:15:40.985 --> 00:15:42.725
Romance languages, German.

00:15:43.355 --> 00:15:47.944
Um, I find the Asian language is more
forgiving when it comes to grammar.

00:15:48.485 --> 00:15:50.855
Uh, it's harder to make a mistake.

00:15:51.395 --> 00:15:52.475
They're more flexible.

00:15:52.475 --> 00:15:53.135
I go today.

00:15:53.135 --> 00:15:53.825
I go tomorrow.

00:15:53.825 --> 00:15:54.755
I go yesterday.

00:15:55.220 --> 00:15:57.710
Um, not quite, but almost.

00:15:57.950 --> 00:16:02.750
In Japanese, there is a clearer
sort of indication and in Korean,

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:10.400
uh, of tense, not so much in
Chinese, uh, no gender, no number.

00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:15.050
Um, yeah, it's, it's, it's easier.

00:16:15.620 --> 00:16:22.220
Uh, this is somewhat compensated or at
least the, the, the counterpart to that is

00:16:22.220 --> 00:16:25.069
the different levels of politeness.

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:31.550
Uh, what the linguistic students call
register, you know, it's, it's quite

00:16:31.550 --> 00:16:35.990
a bit more differentiated than say
in English or in European languages.

00:16:36.470 --> 00:16:38.119
So you have to be a little careful.

00:16:38.659 --> 00:16:43.069
Uh, I therefore try to keep it
neutral in Japanese and Korean.

00:16:43.460 --> 00:16:47.089
Don't wander too far down the
sort of casual side of things.

00:16:47.089 --> 00:16:50.869
And I don't go too far into the
very formal, polite kind of thing.

00:16:51.244 --> 00:16:53.615
Stay neutral until you
get very good at it.

00:16:53.615 --> 00:16:58.234
And then you'll have a sense
of, of what, what form of a word

00:16:58.535 --> 00:17:00.335
verb or whatever is appropriate.

00:17:00.635 --> 00:17:02.345
Less of a problem in Chinese.

00:17:02.675 --> 00:17:07.865
Normally they're not as
polite or as formalized as the

00:17:07.865 --> 00:17:10.504
Japanese and the, uh, Koreans.

00:17:11.135 --> 00:17:16.415
Um, now another issue when learning a
language is the availability of content.

00:17:17.210 --> 00:17:22.250
And, uh, to that extent, I was very
lucky when I studied Mandarin Chinese

00:17:22.910 --> 00:17:27.680
because the Yale in China series had
had such a broad range of material:

00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:33.980
beginner material, um, you know,
books on geography, history with

00:17:34.220 --> 00:17:36.620
500 characters, 1000 characters.

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:41.510
A range of, of, uh, reading
material with glossaries behind

00:17:41.510 --> 00:17:45.980
every chapter or every page, uh,
on literature history, you name it.

00:17:46.820 --> 00:17:51.050
Uh, communist propaganda,
whatever it might be.

00:17:51.379 --> 00:17:52.310
It, it was complete.

00:17:53.180 --> 00:17:55.730
I did not find that to be
the case with Japanese.

00:17:55.730 --> 00:18:00.590
I had to regularly scour the
bookstores to find any kind of reading

00:18:00.590 --> 00:18:02.330
material with, with the glossary.

00:18:02.750 --> 00:18:09.245
Uh, of course easier today because you
can, you can find content, uh, Uh, you

00:18:09.245 --> 00:18:13.084
can go to these automatic transcription
sites, get it transcribed, bring it

00:18:13.084 --> 00:18:14.644
into LingQ, for example, study it.

00:18:14.644 --> 00:18:17.284
So it has become a lot
easier in that regard.

00:18:17.435 --> 00:18:18.034
The same is...

00:18:18.414 --> 00:18:19.624
even worse with Korean.

00:18:19.624 --> 00:18:23.884
One of the reasons why I would sort of
go out Korean for a while and then stop,

00:18:23.884 --> 00:18:28.175
is that there, there is an abundance
of beginner to intermediate material

00:18:28.175 --> 00:18:36.664
in Korean: uninteresting, uninspired
narrators, very difficult to listen to.

00:18:37.264 --> 00:18:44.254
Uh, a lot of emphasis on, you know, older
sister, younger sister, second cousin,

00:18:44.284 --> 00:18:51.665
uncle, third aunt, all the sort of details
of, of different words used for relatives.

00:18:52.024 --> 00:18:53.945
I don't have any Korean relatives.

00:18:54.325 --> 00:18:56.225
To me, that's just...

00:18:56.225 --> 00:18:59.615
and it's part of this general
trend in all three languages,

00:19:00.425 --> 00:19:06.185
those people who create texts, the
native speakers of those languages,

00:19:07.054 --> 00:19:11.344
put a lot of effort into, you know,
festivals and traditional foods and

00:19:11.344 --> 00:19:16.564
things of that nature, which are
therefore less familiar to us and less

00:19:16.564 --> 00:19:20.735
difficult, less, more difficult to get
into, at least as far as I'm concerned.

00:19:21.004 --> 00:19:24.665
I like to hear about how people
lead their everyday lives.

00:19:25.495 --> 00:19:27.625
And of course the, the voice is important.

00:19:27.655 --> 00:19:29.185
It has to have resonance.

00:19:29.185 --> 00:19:31.764
You have to connect with that voice.

00:19:32.185 --> 00:19:36.560
People, natural conversations, uh,
about things in our daily lives

00:19:36.590 --> 00:19:41.659
often, you know, natural conversations
subsequently transcribed, this kind

00:19:41.659 --> 00:19:43.489
of thing is, is, is much better.

00:19:43.489 --> 00:19:48.259
But so in so far as the accessibility
and availability of, of learning

00:19:48.259 --> 00:19:54.259
material, uh, better now than before,
because of our ability to connect to

00:19:54.980 --> 00:20:01.700
netflix and YouTube, people who like
Korean drama or Japanese or Chinese drama

00:20:01.730 --> 00:20:03.770
can find those things to learn from.

00:20:03.800 --> 00:20:10.490
So just trying to touch on some of the
main areas, uh, that influence people

00:20:10.490 --> 00:20:11.899
who are studying these three languages.

00:20:11.899 --> 00:20:13.189
They are all worth it.

00:20:13.220 --> 00:20:19.310
Those are important centers of
a history, of culture, of people

00:20:19.310 --> 00:20:20.659
that we want to get to know.

00:20:21.290 --> 00:20:22.580
They're all a little different.

00:20:22.610 --> 00:20:25.550
They're all well worth
learning for their own reasons.

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:26.540
Okay.

00:20:26.570 --> 00:20:27.350
There you have it.

00:20:27.380 --> 00:20:31.460
And I'm going to leave a couple of
suggested videos, one in Japanese,

00:20:31.460 --> 00:20:34.930
one in Korean that are these one
in Chinese and one, and, uh, in, in

00:20:34.940 --> 00:20:40.930
Japanese that might want to listen to,
to get a sense, uh, of those languages.

00:20:41.590 --> 00:20:41.950
Okay.

00:20:41.950 --> 00:20:42.630
Bye.
For now.

